<!--
  This is an EXAMPLE knowledge file showing the "understanding" layer: not a
  fact or a rule, but the REASONING behind a durable choice. Capturing the
  *why* stops a future session from re-litigating a settled decision or quietly
  undoing it. Copy this to knowledge/why-we-chose-<thing>.md and replace the
  body with a real decision. Delete this comment when you do.

  Keep it to roughly one screen. A rationale doc is read on demand (via search),
  not injected every session — so it can hold the longer-form "story" that does
  not belong in always/ or a one-line topics/ pointer.
-->

# Why we chose X over Y

**Decision:** We use **X** for `<purpose>`. (Replace X/Y with the real options.)

**Context:** What forced the choice and the constraints at the time — e.g. team
size, deadline, existing stack, budget, the skills on hand.

**Options considered:**

- **X** — pro: `<main upside>`; con: `<main downside>`.
- **Y** — pro: `<main upside>`; con: `<the downside that ruled it out>`.

**Why X won:** The one or two factors that actually decided it. Be honest about
the tradeoff accepted, not just the upside.

**What would change this:** The condition under which revisiting Y is warranted
(e.g. "if `<scale threshold>` is crossed" or "if `<constraint>` goes away").
Naming this keeps the decision durable without making it dogma.
